---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Dust and Ashes — Daf Yomi Game: Chullin 88"
  description="Chullin 88: what may you cover slaughtered blood with, and what may you not? Sort sixteen real substances from the mishna and baraita into the right bin, get stranded in the desert and on a ship with no earth in sight, and meet Beit Hillel and Beit Shammai's fight over whether ash even counts as dust."
>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Frank+Ruhl+Libre:wght@400;700;900&display=swap"
  />

  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#0c0f0a] !text-[#e6e8d8]">
    <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
    <div class="border-b border-[#26301c] bg-gradient-to-b from-[#141c0d]/80 to-transparent px-4 pt-10 pb-8 text-center">
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] !text-[#9db35a]">
        <a href="/daf" class="hover:!text-[#c3d97a]">daf yomi arcade</a> · chullin 88 · 2026-07-27
      </p>
      <h1 class="frank text-4xl font-black tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">🏜️ Dust and Ashes</h1>
      <p class="frank mt-1 text-xl !text-[#9db35a]" dir="rtl" lang="he">וְאָנֹכִי עָפָר וָאֵפֶר</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-1 text-xs italic !text-[#93a67e]">"And I am but dust and ashes" — Genesis 18:27, quoted at Chullin 88b:10</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#d3d6bd]">
        The Torah says cover a slaughtered wild animal or bird's blood "with earth" — but what actually counts as
        earth? The mishna lists real substances: some grow plants, some don't; some are technically dust, some
        only look like it. Sort sixteen of them into the right bin, then get stranded without any earth at all —
        once in the desert, once on a ship — and see what the Sages let you use instead.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 pb-16 pt-8">
      <!-- ============ Context card ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#26301c] bg-[#141a0e] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#9db35a]">before you sort</p>
        <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#c3d97a]">🩸 kisui hadam</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a67e]">
              "And he shall pour out its blood and cover it with earth" (Leviticus 17:13) — the mitzva to cover the
              blood of a slaughtered undomesticated animal (<em>chaya</em>) or bird (<em>of</em>) with earth.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#c3d97a]">🌱 RSbG's rule</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a67e]">
              Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel's principle in the mishna: a substance in which plants grow may be used to
              cover the blood; a substance in which plants don't grow may not.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#c3d97a]">📖 the drash</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a67e]">
              "And cover it" is read as a broad term (any substance); "with earth" narrows it back down to things
              that are a <em>min afar</em> — a type of earth — even if they aren't literally soil.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#c3d97a]">🩹 dam hanitaz</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a67e]">
              Blood that spurts onto the wall of the pit, or that remains on the knife. The daf opens with a
              three-way machloket about exactly how much of it you must cover.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Score bar ============ -->
      <div class="sticky top-0 z-10 mb-6 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-5 gap-y-1 rounded-xl border border-[#26301c] bg-[#0c0f0a]/95 px-4 py-2.5 text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] backdrop-blur">
        <span class="!text-[#93a67e]">progress:</span>
        <span id="score-sort" class="!text-[#9db35a]">sorted 0/16</span>
        <span id="score-desert" class="!text-[#9db35a]">desert: unsolved</span>
        <span id="score-ship" class="!text-[#9db35a]">ship: unsolved</span>
        <span id="score-blood" class="!text-[#9db35a]">blood cases 0/2</span>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Activity 0: Whose Blood Counts ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#26301c] bg-[#141a0e] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#9db35a]">warm-up · whose blood counts? — Chullin 88a:4–6</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#d3d6bd]">
          A baraita: blood that spurts onto the wall of the pit, or that stays on the knife, must be covered too.
          Three sages read the word <em>damo</em> ("its blood") differently, and disagree about when you're
          exempt:
        </p>
        <div class="mt-3 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#c3d97a]">the Rabbis</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a67e]"><em>damo</em> = all its blood. Cover every last bit, spurted or not, no matter what else you've already covered.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#c3d97a]">Rabbi Yehuda</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a67e]"><em>damo</em> = even a part of its blood. Covering any blood fulfills the mitzva — spurted blood only needs its own covering if it's the only blood there is.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#c3d97a]">Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a67e]"><em>damo</em> = the special blood — the dam hanefesh, the life-blood of slaughter. Cover that, and you're exempt from the rest.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="blood-case-1" class="blood-case mt-4 rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#e6e8d8]">
            <strong>Case A:</strong> You already covered the main dam hanefesh. Then you notice a few more drops
            that spurted onto the wall of the pit. Must you cover those too?
          </p>
          <div class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-3 gap-2">
            <button class="sage-btn" data-sage="rabbanan" data-value="yes">Rabbis: yes</button>
            <button class="sage-btn" data-sage="yehuda" data-value="no">R' Yehuda: no</button>
            <button class="sage-btn" data-sage="rsbg" data-value="no">RSbG: no</button>
          </div>
          <div class="case-verdict hidden mt-3 rounded-lg border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed"></div>
        </div>

        <div id="blood-case-2" class="blood-case mt-3 rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#e6e8d8]">
            <strong>Case B:</strong> No blood flowed at the slaughter site at all except a few drops that spurted
            onto the knife — that spurted blood is the only blood there is. Must you cover it?
          </p>
          <div class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-3 gap-2">
            <button class="sage-btn" data-sage="rabbanan" data-value="yes">Rabbis: yes</button>
            <button class="sage-btn" data-sage="yehuda" data-value="yes">R' Yehuda: yes</button>
            <button class="sage-btn" data-sage="rsbg" data-value="yes">RSbG: yes</button>
          </div>
          <div class="case-verdict hidden mt-3 rounded-lg border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Activity 1: Sort the Covering ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#26301c] bg-[#141a0e] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#9db35a]">activity 1 · sort the covering — Chullin 88a:7–88b:1</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#d3d6bd]">
          The mishna and the baraita that follows it: some substances count as "earth" for covering the blood,
          some don't. Tap each item, then choose the bin. Every item is real — nothing invented.
        </p>

        <div id="sort-pool" class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2"></div>

        <div class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div>
            <p class="mb-1 text-center text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide !text-[#c3d69a]">מכסין — covers the blood</p>
            <div id="bin-yes" class="min-h-[64px] rounded-xl border border-dashed border-[#3a5a2e] bg-[#0f150a] p-2"></div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="mb-1 text-center text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide !text-[#d9967f]">אין מכסין — does not cover</p>
            <div id="bin-no" class="min-h-[64px] rounded-xl border border-dashed border-[#5a3a2e] bg-[#150f0a] p-2"></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sort-explain" class="mt-3 hidden rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#d3d6bd]"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Activity 2: Stranded ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#26301c] bg-[#141a0e] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#9db35a]">activity 2 · stranded without earth — Chullin 88b:5–9</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#d3d6bd]">
          Two baraitot describe travelers who need to cover blood but have no earth on hand. What do the Sages
          say they should do?
        </p>

        <div id="scene-desert" class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#e6e8d8]">
            🏜️ <strong>Scene 1 — the desert.</strong> You're walking through the wilderness and slaughter an
            undomesticated animal. There's no earth here fit to plant in — nothing sprouts in this sand. You have
            no dirt to cover the blood. What do you do?
          </p>
          <div class="mt-3 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
            <button class="scene-btn" data-scene="desert" data-value="sand">Scoop the desert sand under your feet</button>
            <button class="scene-btn" data-scene="desert" data-value="gold">Grind a gold dinar into powder</button>
            <button class="scene-btn" data-scene="desert" data-value="vessel">Turn your empty water jug over it</button>
            <button class="scene-btn" data-scene="desert" data-value="stones">Pile loose stones over it</button>
          </div>
          <div id="verdict-desert" class="hidden mt-3 rounded-lg border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed"></div>
        </div>

        <div id="scene-ship" class="mt-3 rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#e6e8d8]">
            🚢 <strong>Scene 2 — the ship.</strong> You're crossing the sea and slaughter a bird for a meal. There's
            no earth anywhere aboard. What do you do?
          </p>
          <div class="mt-3 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
            <button class="scene-btn" data-scene="ship" data-value="ash">Burn a corner of your garment, cover with the ash</button>
            <button class="scene-btn" data-scene="ship" data-value="flour">Use flour from the ship's stores</button>
            <button class="scene-btn" data-scene="ship" data-value="filings">Use metal filings from a tool</button>
            <button class="scene-btn" data-scene="ship" data-value="nothing">Leave the blood uncovered — you're exempt at sea</button>
          </div>
          <div id="verdict-ship" class="hidden mt-3 rounded-lg border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed"></div>
        </div>

        <div id="hillel-shammai" class="mt-4 hidden rounded-xl border border-[#1c2414] bg-[#0f130a] p-3">
          <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#c3d97a]">bonus — is ash even "earth"? Chullin 88b:8</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#93a67e]">
            <strong class="!text-[#e6e8d8]">Beit Shammai:</strong> one may not cover except with actual earth —
            ash doesn't qualify. <strong class="!text-[#e6e8d8]">Beit Hillel:</strong> ash does count, since the
            Torah calls the red heifer's ashes "of the earth [<em>me'afar</em>] of the burning" (Numbers 19:17).
            <strong class="!text-[#e6e8d8]">Beit Shammai's comeback:</strong> that verse calls it specifically
            "earth of burning" — not plain "earth" — so it proves nothing about ash on its own.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Activity 3: Aggadta capstone ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#26301c] bg-[#141a0e] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#9db35a]">capstone · Avraham's two mitzvot — Chullin 88b:10–11</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#d3d6bd]">
          Rava: as a reward for Avraham Avinu saying to the angels, "And I am but dust and ashes" (Genesis 18:27),
          his descendants merited two mitzvot built from those very words.
        </p>
        <button id="aggadta-btn" class="mt-3 clue-btn">Reveal the two mitzvot — and why not a third</button>
        <div id="aggadta-text" class="clue-text hidden mt-2"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Learn the real daf ============ -->
      <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-[#26301c] bg-[#141a0e] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#9db35a]">learn the real daf</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#d3d6bd]">
          88a opens by finishing off 87's topic of liquids that issue from ritually impure bodies — a zav's fluids
          are impure by rabbinic decree since people don't naturally avoid him, while a corpse's fluids (besides
          its blood) are not, since people do naturally keep away
          (<a class="!text-[#c3d97a] underline hover:!text-[#dcf29e]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.88a.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">88a:1–3</a>).
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#d3d6bd]">
          Then the daf turns to dam hanitaz — blood that spurts or stays on the knife — and the three-way
          machloket between the Rabbis, Rabbi Yehuda, and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel over how much of it must be
          covered
          (<a class="!text-[#c3d97a] underline hover:!text-[#dcf29e]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.88a.4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">88a:4–6</a>).
          The new mishna follows: what substances may and may not cover blood, capped by Rabban Shimon ben
          Gamliel's grows-plants principle
          (<a class="!text-[#c3d97a] underline hover:!text-[#dcf29e]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.88a.7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">88a:7</a>).
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#d3d6bd]">
          The Gemara defines fine vs. thick sand, then a baraita expands and narrows the list via "and he shall
          cover it" (broad) and "with earth" (narrow), arguing over the correct hermeneutic and landing on
          "a type of earth" as the real test
          (<a class="!text-[#c3d97a] underline hover:!text-[#dcf29e]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.88a.8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">88a:8–88b:3</a>).
          Rav Nachman bar Rav Chisda's grows-plants rule for a chaya or bird's blood gets called nonsense by Rava
          — until the desert-and-ship baraita defends it, followed by the Beit Hillel/Beit Shammai fight over
          whether ash counts as earth, a short list of extra substances (coal dust, stibium, chiseling shavings,
          and some say arsenic), and Rava's closing aggadta about Avraham's "dust and ashes"
          (<a class="!text-[#c3d97a] underline hover:!text-[#dcf29e]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.88b.4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">88b:4–12</a>).
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.88a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#9db35a]/40 bg-[#9db35a]/10 px-3 py-1.5 text-xs font-bold !text-[#c3d97a] hover:bg-[#9db35a]/20">Chullin 88 on Sefaria →</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rashi_on_Chullin.88a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#26301c] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#93a67e] hover:!text-[#e6e8d8]">Rashi (×36)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Steinsaltz_on_Chullin.88a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#26301c] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#93a67e] hover:!text-[#e6e8d8]">Steinsaltz (×22)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Tosafot_on_Chullin.88a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#26301c] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#93a67e] hover:!text-[#e6e8d8]">Tosafot (×10)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Ritva_on_Chullin.88a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#26301c] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#93a67e] hover:!text-[#e6e8d8]">Ritva (×9)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rif_on_Chullin.88a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#26301c] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#93a67e] hover:!text-[#e6e8d8]">Rif (×8)</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <style>
    .frank {
      font-family: 'Frank Ruhl Libre', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    }
    .sage-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a3018;
      background: #0f130a;
      color: #e6e8d8;
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 8px 6px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .sage-btn:hover { border-color: #9db35a; color: #c3d97a; }
    .sage-btn:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.55; }
    .case-verdict.hidden { display: none; }
    .case-verdict.correct { border-color: #7c9152; background: #10160c; color: #c3d69a; }
    .sort-chip {
      border: 1px solid #2a3018;
      background: #0f130a;
      color: #e6e8d8;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 600;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 8px 12px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .sort-chip:hover { border-color: #9db35a; color: #c3d97a; }
    .sort-chip.selected { border-color: #c3d97a; background: #1c260f; color: #dcf29e; }
    .sort-chip.placed-yes { border-color: #7c9152; background: #10160c; color: #c3d69a; cursor: default; }
    .sort-chip.placed-no { border-color: #a75c4a; background: #170e0a; color: #e2a58f; cursor: default; }
    .bin-btn {
      border: 1px dashed #3a5a2e;
      background: transparent;
      color: #c3d97a;
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 4px 10px;
      cursor: pointer;
      margin: 2px;
    }
    .scene-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a3018;
      background: #0f130a;
      color: #e6e8d8;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 600;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 10px 12px;
      cursor: pointer;
      text-align: left;
    }
    .scene-btn:hover { border-color: #9db35a; color: #c3d97a; }
    .scene-btn:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.5; }
    #verdict-desert.hidden, #verdict-ship.hidden { display: none; }
    .correct-scene { border-color: #7c9152 !important; background: #10160c; color: #c3d69a !important; }
    .wrong-scene { border-color: #a75c4a !important; background: #170e0a; color: #e2a58f !important; }
    .clue-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a3018;
      background: #0f130a;
      color: #c3d97a;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 10px 14px;
      cursor: pointer;
      text-align: left;
    }
    .clue-btn:hover { border-color: #9db35a; }
    .clue-btn:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.6; }
    .clue-text {
      border: 1px solid #1c2414;
      background: #0f130a;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 10px 14px;
      font-size: 12px;
      line-height: 1.6;
      color: #d3d6bd;
    }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .fade-in { animation: fadeIn 0.3s ease both; }
      @keyframes fadeIn { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(4px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: none; } }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id);
    const $$ = (sel: string, root: ParentNode = document) => Array.from(root.querySelectorAll(sel));

    // ==================================================================
    // Warm-up — Whose Blood Counts, Chullin 88a:4-6. Three sages read
    // "damo" differently: the Rabbis (all of it), Rabbi Yehuda (even
    // part of it satisfies), Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel (the special
    // dam hanefesh).
    // ==================================================================
    const CASE_EXPLAIN: Record<string, string> = {
      'blood-case-1': '<strong>The Rabbis say yes</strong> — "its blood" means every last drop, so the spurted blood needs its own covering regardless of what you\'ve already done. <strong>Rabbi Yehuda says no</strong> — covering the main blood already fulfilled "damo" (even a part of its blood suffices), and the rule only makes spurted/knife blood a separate obligation "when there is no blood except that blood." <strong>Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says no</strong> — his condition is explicit: "if one already covered the blood of the soul, he is exempt from covering" the rest (Chullin 88a:4-5).',
      'blood-case-2': '<strong>All three agree: yes.</strong> When the spurted or knife blood is the only blood there is, everyone requires covering it — the Rabbis because it\'s still "all its blood," Rabbi Yehuda because "there is no blood except that blood" (his own stated exception no longer applies), and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel because the dam hanefesh itself was never covered (Chullin 88a:4-5).',
    };
    let bloodCasesDone = 0;
    $$('.blood-case').forEach((card) => {
      let answered = false;
      $$('.sage-btn', card).forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (answered) return;
          answered = true;
          $$('.sage-btn', card).forEach((b) => ((b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true));
          $$('.sage-btn', card).forEach((b) => {
            (b as HTMLElement).style.borderColor = '#7c9152';
            (b as HTMLElement).style.color = '#c3d69a';
          });
          const verdict = card.querySelector('.case-verdict');
          if (verdict) {
            verdict.classList.remove('hidden');
            verdict.classList.add('correct', 'fade-in');
            verdict.innerHTML = CASE_EXPLAIN[card.id] || '';
          }
          bloodCasesDone += 1;
          const scoreEl = $('score-blood');
          if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `blood cases ${bloodCasesDone}/2`;
        });
      });
    });

    // ==================================================================
    // Activity 1 — Sort the Covering, Chullin 88a:7-88b:1. The mishna's
    // list plus the baraita's expansion/exclusion via "vechisahu" (broad)
    // and "be'afar" (narrow: only a min afar qualifies).
    // ==================================================================
    type Item = { label: string; he: string; bin: 'yes' | 'no'; ref: string; why: string };
    const ITEMS: Item[] = [
      { label: 'Fine sand', he: 'חוֹל הַדַּק', bin: 'yes', ref: 'Chullin 88a:7-8', why: 'Sand the potter doesn\'t need to crush — a min afar, and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\'s "grows plants" test holds.' },
      { label: 'Fine manure', he: 'זֶבֶל הַדַּק', bin: 'yes', ref: 'Chullin 88a:7', why: 'Listed first in the mishna — literally what plants grow in.' },
      { label: 'Lime', he: 'סִיד', bin: 'yes', ref: 'Chullin 88a:7', why: 'The mishna\'s list includes it outright.' },
      { label: 'Crushed potsherd / clay', he: 'חַרְסִית', bin: 'yes', ref: 'Chullin 88a:7', why: 'A crushed, earth-like substance — counted as min afar.' },
      { label: 'Crushed brick or jar-stopper', he: 'לְבֵנָה וּמְגוּפָה שֶׁכְּתָשָׁן', bin: 'yes', ref: 'Chullin 88a:7', why: 'Once pounded down to dust, it behaves like earth. Left whole, it does not — see the "no" bin.' },
      { label: 'Crushed stones', he: 'שְׁחִיקַת אֲבָנִים', bin: 'yes', ref: 'Chullin 88a:10', why: 'The baraita includes stones once they\'re ground to powder — a min afar, unlike whole stones.' },
      { label: 'Fine flax chaff', he: 'נְעוֹרֶת פִּשְׁתָּן דַּקָּה', bin: 'yes', ref: 'Chullin 88a:10', why: 'Fine plant refuse makes the baraita\'s "included" list.' },
      { label: 'Stibium (kohl powder)', he: 'כְּחוֹל', bin: 'yes', ref: 'Chullin 88b:9', why: 'Added by a later baraita to the list of usable substances.' },
      { label: 'Thick sand', he: 'חוֹל הַגַּס', bin: 'no', ref: 'Chullin 88a:7-8', why: 'Sand the potter must crush before use — too coarse to count as earth, and it doesn\'t sustain growth.' },
      { label: 'Thick manure', he: 'זֶבֶל הַגַּס', bin: 'no', ref: 'Chullin 88a:7', why: 'The mishna explicitly excludes it — too coarse.' },
      { label: 'Uncrushed brick or jar-stopper', he: 'לְבֵנָה וּמְגוּפָה שֶׁלֹּא כְּתָשָׁן', bin: 'no', ref: 'Chullin 88a:7', why: 'Whole, it\'s not earth-like — only crushed does it qualify.' },
      { label: 'Whole, uncrushed stones', he: 'אֲבָנִים', bin: 'no', ref: 'Chullin 88a:10', why: 'The very first thing the drash rejects: "one might have thought he may cover with stones" — no, only earth.' },
      { label: 'Flour', he: 'קֶמַח', bin: 'no', ref: 'Chullin 88b:1', why: 'A food product, not a type of earth — explicitly excluded.' },
      { label: 'Bran', he: 'סוּבִּין', bin: 'no', ref: 'Chullin 88b:1', why: 'Excluded alongside flour — not min afar.' },
      { label: 'Metal filings', he: 'שְׁחִיקַת כְּלֵי מַתָּכוֹת', bin: 'no', ref: 'Chullin 88b:1', why: 'Ground fine, but metal isn\'t earth — excluded by the baraita.' },
      { label: 'Just flip a vessel over it', he: 'כּוֹפֶה עָלָיו אֶת הַכְּלִי', bin: 'no', ref: 'Chullin 88a:7, 88a:10', why: 'Not a substance at all — the mishna and the drash both reject merely covering the blood from view without a min afar touching it.' },
    ];

    let selected: { item: Item; chip: HTMLElement } | null = null;
    let sortedCount = 0;

    function selectChip(item: Item, chip: HTMLElement) {
      if (chip.classList.contains('placed-yes') || chip.classList.contains('placed-no')) return;
      $$('.sort-chip').forEach((c) => c.classList.remove('selected'));
      chip.classList.add('selected');
      selected = { item, chip };
    }

    function placeInBin(bin: 'yes' | 'no') {
      if (!selected) return;
      const { item, chip } = selected;
      const correct = item.bin === bin;
      chip.classList.remove('selected');
      chip.classList.add(correct ? 'placed-yes' : 'placed-no');
      chip.textContent = `${item.label} (${item.bin === 'yes' ? 'covers' : 'does not cover'})`;
      const targetBin = $(bin === 'yes' ? 'bin-yes' : 'bin-no');
      if (targetBin) targetBin.appendChild(chip);

      const explain = $('sort-explain');
      if (explain) {
        explain.classList.remove('hidden');
        explain.classList.add('fade-in');
        explain.innerHTML = `${correct ? '<strong class="!text-[#c3d69a]">Correct.</strong>' : `<strong class="!text-[#e2a58f]">Actually it belongs in "${item.bin === 'yes' ? 'covers' : 'does not cover'}."</strong>`} <strong>${item.label}</strong> (<span dir="rtl" lang="he">${item.he}</span>) — ${item.why} <span class="!text-[#93a67e]">(${item.ref})</span>`;
      }
      selected = null;
      sortedCount += 1;
      const scoreEl = $('score-sort');
      if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `sorted ${sortedCount}/${ITEMS.length}`;
    }

    const pool = $('sort-pool');
    if (pool) {
      ITEMS.forEach((item) => {
        const chip = document.createElement('button');
        chip.className = 'sort-chip';
        chip.textContent = item.label;
        chip.addEventListener('click', () => selectChip(item, chip));
        pool.appendChild(chip);
      });
    }
    $('bin-yes')?.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      if ((e.target as HTMLElement).id === 'bin-yes') placeInBin('yes');
    });
    $('bin-no')?.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      if ((e.target as HTMLElement).id === 'bin-no') placeInBin('no');
    });
    // Also allow placing by clicking the bin label area directly after selecting.
    ['bin-yes', 'bin-no'].forEach((id) => {
      $(id)?.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (selected) placeInBin(id === 'bin-yes' ? 'yes' : 'no');
      });
    });

    // ==================================================================
    // Activity 2 — Stranded Without Earth, Chullin 88b:5-9. Desert:
    // grind a gold dinar (Rabbi Zeira: Job 28:6, "afrot zahav"). Ship:
    // burn a garment, cover with ash (Beit Hillel: Numbers 19:17).
    // ==================================================================
    const SCENE_EXPLAIN: Record<string, Record<string, string>> = {
      desert: {
        gold: '<strong class="!text-[#c3d69a]">Correct.</strong> The baraita: "If one is traveling in the desert... and does not have dirt with which to cover the blood, he may grind a gold dinar into powder and cover the blood with it." Rabbi Zeira derives that gold counts as "dust" from Job 28:6, "and it has dust of gold" (Chullin 88b:6-7).',
        sand: '<strong class="!text-[#e2a58f]">Not quite.</strong> The Gemara concludes that true desert sand — the kind that lets nothing sprout — doesn\'t count as earth for this mitzva at all: "desert sand, which does not allow for the sprouting of seeds, may not be used." That\'s exactly why the traveler needs the gold dinar instead (Chullin 88b:6).',
        vessel: '<strong class="!text-[#e2a58f]">No good.</strong> The mishna already rejected simply inverting a vessel over the blood — that\'s not covering it with any substance at all (Chullin 88a:7).',
        stones: '<strong class="!text-[#e2a58f]">No good.</strong> The drash explicitly rejects covering with stones: "one might have thought he may cover with stones... the verse states: with earth" (Chullin 88a:10).',
      },
      ship: {
        ash: '<strong class="!text-[#c3d69a]">Correct.</strong> The same baraita continues: "If one is traveling on a ship... he may burn his garment and cover the blood with the ashes." Ash is called "afar" per Beit Hillel\'s proof from the red heifer\'s ashes (Numbers 19:17) — see the bonus below (Chullin 88b:6, 88b:8).',
        flour: '<strong class="!text-[#e2a58f]">No good.</strong> The baraita explicitly excludes flour from the substances that count as earth (Chullin 88b:1).',
        filings: '<strong class="!text-[#e2a58f]">No good.</strong> Metal filings are explicitly excluded too — ground fine, but not a min afar (Chullin 88b:1).',
        nothing: '<strong class="!text-[#e2a58f]">Not exempt.</strong> The mitzva still applies at sea — the baraita gives you a real solution (burn your garment for ash) rather than exempting you (Chullin 88b:6).',
      },
    };
    ['desert', 'ship'].forEach((scene) => {
      let answered = false;
      $$(`.scene-btn[data-scene="${scene}"]`).forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (answered) return;
          answered = true;
          const val = btn.getAttribute('data-value') || '';
          const correctVal = scene === 'desert' ? 'gold' : 'ash';
          $$(`.scene-btn[data-scene="${scene}"]`).forEach((b) => {
            (b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true;
            if (b.getAttribute('data-value') === correctVal) b.classList.add('correct-scene');
          });
          if (val !== correctVal) btn.classList.add('wrong-scene');
          const verdict = $(`verdict-${scene}`);
          if (verdict) {
            verdict.classList.remove('hidden');
            verdict.classList.add('fade-in');
            verdict.innerHTML = SCENE_EXPLAIN[scene][val] || '';
          }
          const scoreEl = $(`score-${scene}`);
          if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `${scene}: ${val === correctVal ? 'solved' : 'missed — see why'}`;
          if (scene === 'desert' && $('score-ship')) {
            // no-op, just keeping structure symmetric
          }
          const hs = $('hillel-shammai');
          if (hs && scene === 'ship') {
            hs.classList.remove('hidden');
            hs.classList.add('fade-in');
          }
        });
      });
    });

    // ==================================================================
    // Capstone — Chullin 88b:10-11. Rava: Avraham's "dust and ashes"
    // earned the ashes of the red heifer and the dust of the sotah —
    // not the dust of covering the blood, since that's an accessory to
    // the mitzva with no direct benefit (hana'ah).
    // ==================================================================
    $('aggadta-btn')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const btn = $('aggadta-btn') as HTMLButtonElement;
      const text = $('aggadta-text');
      btn.disabled = true;
      if (text) {
        text.classList.remove('hidden');
        text.classList.add('fade-in');
        text.innerHTML = 'Rava: "As reward for that which our Patriarch Abraham said: \'And I am but dust and ashes,\' his children merited two mitzvot: the ashes of the red heifer, and the dust of the sotah" (Chullin 88b:10). The Gemara asks: why not count the dust used to cover the blood as a third? Rava\'s answer: covering the blood is a <em>hechsher mitzva</em> — an accessory that enables the mitzva to be done properly — but it provides no direct benefit (<em>hana\'ah</em>) the way the red heifer\'s ashes purify the impure, or the sotah\'s dust brings a wrongly-suspected wife back to her husband in peace (Chullin 88b:11).';
      }
    });
  </script>
</Layout>
